<p>I have spent much of the last three years building our life around home and family. I chose to be here for these years, and I am deeply grateful that I could. There were very long days and nights involved. Unexpectedly difficult days and plenty of ordinary ones. But looking back now, it is the ordinary parts I already know I will miss most.</p>

<p>Not because I want to keep everything as it is. I can’t wait to meet the girl Daisy is becoming. I want to hear the things she has to tell me when she comes home. I want her to have places she loves that have nothing to do with me.</p>

<p>Maybe that is one of the strange things about raising children.<br />I spend years making their world feel safe and familiar, and then, little by little, I watch them make it bigger.</p>

<p>Our days at home won’t disappear when playgroup starts. She will still come running into the kitchen. There will still be toys under the table and half-eaten apples left in places where half-eaten apples have no business being. Rosie is still little, and our home will continue to be full of all the things that come with this season of life.</p>

<p>But something will be different. There will be a few hours each week that are hers. And perhaps that is why this small change feels bigger than it looks from the outside.</p>

<p>Three years ago, her whole world fit inside ours.<br />Now she is beginning to build a little world of her own.<br />And I have the privilege of watching it grow.</p>
